latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the Input Grid:\n   a. Calculate color frequencies for the entire grid and the central area.\n   b. Identify the most dominant colors.\n   c. Analyze color transitions and relationships.\n   d. Detect any repeating patterns or structures, especially in the central area.\n\n2. Determine Output Size:\n   a. Based on the complexity of the input pattern, decide on an appropriate output size.\n   b. More complex patterns should result in larger outputs, while simpler patterns can be represented in smaller grids.\n   c. Set a minimum (e.g., 3x3) and maximum (e.g., 8x8) size for the output.\n\n3. Extract Core Pattern:\n   a. Focus on the central area of the input grid.\n   b. Identify key structural elements or repeating patterns.\n   c. Determine the minimum area that captures the essence of the pattern.\n\n4. Create Color Palette:\n   a. Select the most important colors based on frequency and their role in the pattern.\n   b. Ensure that colors crucial to the pattern's structure are included, even if not the most frequent.\n\n5. Generate Initial Output Grid:\n   a. Create an empty grid of the determined output size.\n   b. Place key structural elements or pattern components in their relative positions.\n\n6. Fill in Details:\n   a. Use the color palette to fill in the remaining cells.\n   b. Maintain color relationships and transitions observed in the input.\n   c. Ensure the overall color distribution is representative of the input.\n\n7. Refine Output:\n   a. Compare the generated output with the input, focusing on pattern similarity.\n   b. Adjust colors or patterns if necessary to better capture the input's essence.\n   c. Ensure that the output maintains key characteristics of the input pattern.\n\n8. Validate Output:\n   a. Check that the output grid meets size constraints (smaller than input, within set limits).\n   b. Verify that it captures the core pattern and color relationships of the input.\n   c. Ensure it represents a meaningful compression of the input data.\n\n9. Optimize (if necessary):\n   a. If the initial output doesn't satisfactorily represent the input, consider adjusting the output size or color selection.\n   b. Iterate through steps 5-8 with the new parameters.\n\n10. Return Result:\n    a. Once satisfied with the output, return the final ColoredGrid object.\n\nThis plan aims to create a flexible algorithm that can adapt to various input patterns, producing outputs that efficiently capture the essence of each unique input grid. The key is balancing between compression (reducing size) and information retention (keeping essential pattern details)."
